ing  feature is going to get the information  out of the data and pass it on to the  labeling feature and that's going to  decide how it wants to label these  different points so you have these an  amount of points in the last plot over  here and then the feature the labeling  feature is going to come in and see how  to label these points which ones to  label where to put the label so that  they can avoid collisions and you know  adding automatic tooltips and so on the  second example that I have data handling  is quantities Bolton language is  probably one of the very few languages  that natively supports units so we want  to be able to take advantage of that so  here are a couple of a few plots and we  are feeding them data that have units  attached to them and so we have the 2d  and 3d versions of smooth histogram and  they're able to detect the units and put  them on the axes as labels we also have  deeper integration of units with our  plots so if you look at the last  plotless line plot it has it has data  which is units attached and you can also  specify target units here which is in  feet and the visualisation functions  natively do the transformations on those  units and then plot with the transformed  units so you can see feet being  displayed on the y-axis for that plot  the last example that I have here is  weighted data and there are two types of  plots here you have a couple of  statistical plots up on the top and then  lists line plot at the bottom and we  feed in the weighted data to quantile  plot which is plotting quantile against  quantile and and it's looking at the  data and making the determination that  it can use those weights in the internal  computations that it's doing and so  that's it uses that weighted data and  then gives you the plot on the other  hand this line plot is being fed the  weighted data as well but it thinks it  there is nothing interesting that can do  with those weights so it's going to  ignore those weights and just plo